<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.10.3: Converting from Standard Form to Vertex Form</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02500</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.10.3: Converting from Standard Form to Vertex Form</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5caab281-1fec-460c-aa50-c42d7eaabb9c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>You've expanded from factored form to standard form. To learn how to convert from standard form to <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">vertex form (of a quadratic expression)</term>, start by going backward and convert a quadratic from vertex form to standard form.</para>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Rewrite \((x+5)^2+1\) in standard form.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(x^2+10x+26\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Rewrite \((x−3)^2−7\) in standard form.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(x^2−6x+2\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Think about the steps you took, and about reversing them. Try converting one or both of the expressions in standard form back into vertex form. Explain how you go about converting the expressions.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>To convert \(x^2−6x+2\) back to vertex form, you can complete the square by adding 9 and then subtracting 9 so that the expression remains unchanged. The part that is a perfect square, \(x^2−6x+9\), can be written as \((x−3)^2\), and then there is the \(2−9\), which equals -7.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Test your strategy by rewriting \(x^2+10x+9\) in vertex form.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\((x+5)^2−16\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Let's check the expression you rewrote in vertex form. Use the Desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course to graph both \(x^2+10x+9\) and your new expression.</item>
</list>

<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>

<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="2f17a093-e144-48ae-879e-5aac2e5b3744"><problem><para>
      <para>Does it appear that both expressions define the same function?</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>Yes</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is Yes.Both expressions seem to produce the same graph.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>If you convert your expression in vertex form back into standard form, do you get \(x^2+10x+9\)?</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para> \((x+5)^2−16=x^2+10x+25−16\), which is \(x^2+10x+9\).</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
